  ('6ec2d684-8091-4da9-8814-8a3052d0a302', 'Side-Kick Breathing (Kickboard)', 'Glutes', 'Push off the wall on your side (not flat on your stomach), one arm extended forward holding the board or just extended, kicking continuously. Keep your head in a neutral position looking at the bottom of the pool, and when you need air, rotate your head sideways (one ear stays in the water, other faces up) to breathe, then rotate back to neutral. Swim a length like this, then repeat on the other side. This teaches the exact head position and rotation you''ll use once full stroke breathing comes in.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Imported from swim plan', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2484/view/'),
  ('d9c1f78c-7bf6-4e6a-92e4-7bf4ed4a0a67', 'Side-laying interior rotation', 'Trapezius', 'Laying on your side and using the arm closest to the bench, maintain a 90 degree bend at the elbow and allow the weight to fall towards the ground past the bench as low as comfortable. Bring the weight up using only rotation of the shoulder to the opposing shoulder.', 'Barbell, Bench, Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'CptnFatbeard', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1582/view/'),
  ('d9f6c15a-b224-4ef6-93e9-fd6509b66add', 'Side-lying External Rotation', null, 'With a weight in one hand, lie on your side opposite the weight. Keep your knees slightly bent. Hold your elbow against your side, and extend your upper arm straight ahead of you. While continuing to hold your elbow against your side, rotate your upper arm 90 degrees upwards.
It is helpful to place a towel under your armpit to help with the form on this exercise. Placing a support under your head for the duration of the exercise is also a good idea.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'apeschel', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/578/view/'),
  ('768e0703-a04d-4d97-89ce-a49cd6be2b06', 'Single Arm Plank to Row', 'Shoulders, Biceps, Brachialis, Lats, Obliquus externus abdominis, Abs, Serratus anterior', 'Start position as row, extend to plank and back. Finish with row and repeat', 'Kettlebell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1022/view/'),
  ('81dd44b3-67b3-4198-9e57-c2a80b3c4102', 'Single arm row', 'Lats', 'Sitting on the ground, use a high cable in a single grip, to do lat pulldowns with a focus on a long stretch in the lats.', 'Cable machine, Incline bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Rottekongen', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1637/view/'),
  ('046fce45-69f2-46f7-a5b6-79a25a485af1', 'Single Leg Extension', 'Quads', null, null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/71/view/'),
  ('68f1efb8-e7d2-4a79-99bb-65f128e10a11', 'Single Leg Glute Bridge', 'Glutes', 'A glute bridge, where you use just one leg at a time.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Happy', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1740/view/'),
  ('45cd73d7-98ac-4451-849a-a645fcfe9bee', 'Single Leg Hamstring Stretch', 'Hamstrings', 'Sit on the floor with one leg extended straight in front of you, toes pointing up. Bend the other leg and rest the sole of that foot against the inner thigh of the straight leg, letting the knee fall out to the side.
Sit up tall with your chest open and your shoulders back. Breathe in, then as you breathe out, lean forward from the hips over the straight leg while keeping your back long. Reach for your shin, ankle or foot, only as far as you can without rounding your back, and hold. Then switch sides.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1393/view/'),
  ('09c9a566-c1b3-4bf6-8a48-f9dd2bbec1f2', 'Single leg press', 'Hamstrings, Calves, Glutes, Quads', 'Strength training exercise where you push a weight away from your body using one leg at a time on a leg press machine. It helps to improve leg strength, balance, and muscular symmetry by isolating each leg individually. This exercise is particularly useful for identifying and correcting muscle imbalances between your legs.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'qoxivi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2677/view/'),
  ('957ca37c-b6d7-4c30-8ba9-9512b0fa2659', 'Single Leg RDL', 'Hamstrings', '- Stand upright and hold weights in both hands if using loads.
- Brace your core and lift one leg off the ground.
- Keep your back straight, hinge at the hips while lowering your torso forward, ensuring you don’t rotate your hips.
- Lower until you feel a stretch in your standing leg''s hamstring, then return to standing position. Repeat on both sides.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1388/view/'),
  ('83af8d94-319b-456b-92d8-75cec821a195', 'Single-arm cable pushdown', 'Triceps', 'Single-arm cable pushdown is a unilateral isolation exercise that targets the triceps brachii. By working one arm at a time, it helps correct strength imbalances between sides while also engaging the core for stability.
Starting position:
Stand facing the cable machine with your feet shoulder-width apart. Attach a single handle to the high pulley. Grab the handle with one hand using an overhand grip (pronated), with your elbow bent at approximately 90° and tucked close to your body. Keep your core braced and your back straight.
Execution:
Push the handle downward in a controlled motion by extending your elbow until your arm is fully extended. Hold the contraction for 1 second at the bottom, then slowly return to the starting position.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'evtimovgeorg', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1969/view/'),
  ('0cb0c5aa-f3dc-4883-b181-0b5efda1bc4f', 'Single-Arm Cable Row with Rotation (Floor)', 'Lats', 'Go into a one-legged kneeling position and grab the cable from the lowest position.
Rotate the upper torso to get into the starting position and bend the upper torso a little bit sidewise.
Place the unused hand on the knee.
Pull the cable while rotating the upper torso.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'bbk', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2663/view/'),
  ('9dfce8f0-39fc-4204-9f84-500ec42074e9', 'Single-arm dumbbell shoulder press', 'Shoulders', 'Press the dumbbell vertically overhead in a controlled motion until your arm is fully extended, without locking the elbow. Slowly lower the dumbbell back to the starting position at shoulder height.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'evtimovgeorg', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1968/view/'),
  ('b86fd8df-e726-4998-a7ee-000de0f70bf0', 'Single-Arm Lat Pulldown', 'Lats, Trapezius', 'Sit at the lat pulldown machine and grab the single handle with a neutral grip. Keep your torso stable, your chest lifted, and your shoulder blades slightly retracted. Start with your arm fully extended overhead, then pull the handle down toward your upper chest following a controlled, slightly diagonal path. Focus on engaging the latissimus dorsi while keeping the shoulder depressed and avoiding any torso rotation. Slowly return to the starting position, maintaining full control throughout the movement.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'evtimovgeorg', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1972/view/'),
  ('8aa52a2f-a9ee-4740-9623-9fef145b2ca3', 'Single-Arm Lat Pulldown (Custom)', 'Lats, Trapezius', 'Perform Single-Arm Lat Pulldown with controlled form, keeping tension on the target muscles, pausing briefly at the peak contraction, then returning slowly under control without using momentum.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ruochenma', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2722/view/'),
  ('c85e6137-2577-4e28-82c2-427407d534eb', 'Single-arm Preacher Curl', 'Biceps', 'Sit on the preacher curl bench and perform a bicep curl with a dumbbell in one hand. Your other hand can be at rest, or beneath your curling arm''s elbow.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/584/view/'),
  ('f9880802-e5bd-4143-97f6-d0ee82ba9dd2', 'Single-Leg Balance with Eyes Closed', null, 'A balance and proprioception drill. Stand on one leg with the knee slightly bent and the other foot lifted clear of the floor, then close your eyes and hold the position. Without visual feedback the ankle and hip stabilisers have to do the work, so expect noticeable wobble. Stand within reach of a wall, counter or chair so you can steady yourself if needed. Hold for the prescribed time, then switch legs. Commonly prescribed in rehabilitation after ankle or knee injury.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'trs1800', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2667/view/'),
  ('d229de41-3533-489f-8460-08a6e3daa25a', 'Single-Leg Balance with Kettlebell Circles', 'Glutes', 'A balance drill that adds a moving load. Stand on one leg with the knee slightly bent, holding a kettlebell in one hand. Pass the kettlebell around your body in a circle, handing it from one hand to the other in front and behind you, while keeping your balance on the standing leg. Keep the hips level and the torso upright; the shifting weight of the bell constantly changes your centre of mass, which is what trains the hip and ankle stabilisers. Complete the prescribed number of circles, reverse direction, then switch legs. Use a light bell to start.', 'Kettlebell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'trs1800', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2668/view/'),
  ('53b8727f-ad5c-402a-83e6-7b8e9c0bd99a', 'Single-leg BOSU balance', null, 'Single-Leg BOSU Balance Exercise
The exercise for balancing on a BOSU ball while standing on one leg is known as the single-leg BOSU balance exercise. This exercise is effective for enhancing balance, coordination, and core stability.
Benefits of the Single-Leg BOSU Balance Exercise
- Improves Balance: Standing on one leg challenges your stability.
- Enhances Coordination: Engaging different muscle groups helps improve overall coordination.
- Strengthens Core: The exercise activates core muscles, promoting better posture and stability.
How to Perform the Single-Leg BOSU Balance Exercise
- Positioning: Stand on the dome side of the BOSU ball with one leg.
- Balance: Keep your opposite leg lifted and your core engaged.
- Duration: Aim to hold the position for 10-20 seconds, gradually increasing the time as you improve.
Progression Tips
- Add Movement: Try reaching for objects around you while maintaining balance.
- Close Your Eyes: This increases the challenge by removing visual stability cues.
- Use a Ball: Catch or toss a ball while balancing to further engage your core and coordination.
This exercise is a great way to build foundational balance skills before progressing to more complex movements.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'aboksz', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2616/view/'),
  ('a630de17-9170-434c-b486-96a6b8705506', 'Single-Leg Deadlift with Dumbbell', 'Hamstrings, Glutes', 'Starting Position: Stand upright with a slight bend in your knees, holding a dumbbell in one or both hands. Shift your weight onto your supporting leg (e.g., your right leg) and lift your non-supporting foot slightly off the floor.
The Hinge: Keeping your back flat and core engaged, hinge at your hips, pushing your glutes backward. As your torso lowers, extend your non-supporting leg straight back behind you for balance, keeping your hips square to the floor. The dumbbell(s) should lower toward the ground along the line of your supporting leg.
Range of Motion: Continue lowering your torso until it is nearly parallel to the floor, or you feel an intense stretch in your supporting hamstring, ensuring your back remains neutral throughout the movement.
Return to Start: Pause at the bottom, then contract your glutes and hamstrings to slowly raise your torso back to the starting position. Your non-supporting leg should return in line with the supporting one.
Switch Sides: After completing your desired number of repetitions on one side, switch your weight and repeat the movement on the other leg.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1736/view/'),
  ('caccd791-ff30-452a-816e-790edadb78cd', 'Single-leg hamstring curl', 'Hamstrings', 'Specifically, the muscles involved are: biceps femoris, semimembranosus and semitendinosus.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1294/view/'),
  ('ff6c2fb1-5105-49ba-babe-c0d403940269', 'Single-Leg Lunge with Kettlebell:', 'Calves, Glutes, Quads', 'How to do a single-leg kettlebell lunge
Preparation: Stand with your feet together. Hold the kettlebell with both hands by the handle, keeping it close to your chest, or hold it with one hand (on the same side as the working leg).
Step and Lower: Take a large step forward (or backward, which is safer for your knees) with one leg, lowering your hips until both knees form a 90-degree angle.
Focus: Shift your weight onto your front (or bent) leg, keeping your back straight and your torso upright.
Return: Push off with your front leg to return to the starting position.', 'Kettlebell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1734/view/'),
  ('d28cf04f-4077-484b-82ed-abaea8d4053d', 'Single-leg side glute press', 'Calves, Glutes, Quads', 'The Single-Leg Side Glute Press is a leg press machine variation where you angle your body sideways on the pad, placing one foot high and slightly angled to press the weight, powerfully targeting the outer glute (gluteus medius/minimus) and hamstrings for balanced leg development and stability, requiring focus on pushing through the heel and maintaining control without locking out.
How to Perform:
Setup: Sit in the leg press machine, rotate your body to one hip, placing one foot high on the platform at about a 45-degree angle with toes pointed slightly out/forward.
Positioning: Keep your hip, knee, and ankle aligned, and ensure your lower back stays on the pad.
Descent: Slowly lower the weight, allowing your knee to bend deeply (near 90 degrees), feeling a stretch in your glute and hamstring.
Press: Drive through your heel (not your toes) to press the weight back up, squeezing your glutes and hamstrings, stopping just before your leg fully extends.
Control: Avoid bouncing and don''t let your lower back lift off the pad; maintain tension throughout the movement.', 'Incline bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1735/view/'),
  ('eb8d3fa8-8cfc-4961-8e3d-5bf0a5081797', 'Single-Leg Soft-Landing Holds', null, 'Single-Leg Soft-Landing Holds
Single-leg soft-landing holds are exercises that focus on balance and stability by landing softly on one leg and holding the position. This exercise helps improve proprioception and can be beneficial for rehabilitation and athletic training.
Overview
Single-leg soft-landing holds are exercises designed to enhance balance, stability, and proprioception. They involve landing softly on one leg and maintaining that position for a brief period. This exercise is particularly useful in rehabilitation settings and for athletes looking to improve their performance.
Benefits
- Improves balance: Enhances stability by training the body to control weight distribution on one leg.
- Enhances proprioception: Increases awareness of body position, which is crucial for injury prevention.
- Supports rehabilitation: Helps individuals recover from lower-limb injuries by strengthening muscles and improving joint stability.
How to Perform Single-Leg Soft-Landing Holds
Steps
- Preparation: Stand on a stable surface or a box.
- Takeoff: Step off the box or surface with one leg, ensuring that you do not jump.
- Landing: Land softly on the target leg, allowing the knee and hip to bend slightly.
- Hold position: Maintain balance in the landed position for up to 2 seconds.
- Reset: Return to the starting position and repeat.
Key Points to Remember
- Keep your chest up and engage your core for better stability.
- Ensure your knee aligns with the midline of your foot during landing.
- Focus on a controlled descent to minimize impact.
Conclusion
Incorporating single-leg soft-landing holds into your training routine can significantly enhance your balance and stability, making it a valuable exercise for both rehabilitation and athletic performance.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'aboksz', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2619/view/'),
  ('5a5e8883-b185-40ff-b292-9e433d13b5e7', 'Sit & Reach', 'Hamstrings', 'Sit on the ground with your legs extended straight in front of you. Reach both hands forward, trying to reach past your toes or as far as you can go. Return to the starting position and repeat.', 'Gym mat', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1394/view/'),
  ('5af00591-2d77-4ea6-a347-825ad18beff1', 'Sit Up Elbow Thrust', 'Obliquus externus abdominis, Abs', 'This
is a top down rotation movement, and it’s a bit more
explosive, too.
Lying with knees bent and feet on the floor, sit up and then drive your left elbow across your entire body toward the right, then come back
to center and then finally lower yourself down. Then hit the other side.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Moffi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1479/view/'),
  ('f38e9c23-031d-44d0-ac27-7f1026212c73', 'Sit-ups', 'Abs', 'Sit on a mat, your calves are resting on a bench, the knees make a right angle. Hold your hands behind your neck. Go now up with a rolling movement of your back, you should feel how the individual vertebrae lose contact with the mat. At the highest point, contract your abs as much as you can and hold there for 2 sec. Go now down, unrolling your back.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/591/view/'),
  ('6e46833d-fd83-4c1a-90af-eb3f3a917199', 'Sitting Calf Raises', 'Soleus', 'Sit on a bench for calf raises and check that the feet are half free and that you can completely stretch the calf muscles down. Pull your calves up, going as far (up) as you can. Make at the highest point a short pause of 1 or 2 seconds and go down.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/590/view/'),
  ('9f9bfa54-4702-4eb1-a72a-0fc12c846c72', 'Sitting Calf Stretch (Dorsiflexion)', 'Calves, Soleus', 'This is a light stretch for the calf that is great for rehab.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'erikocobra', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1274/view/'),
  ('e29d9914-6b02-490b-8f3a-f9fe8f21993c', 'Ski Machine', 'Calves, Lats, Trapezius, Triceps', '- Start standing on the platform with your feet hip-width apart.
- Reach overhead to grip the handles with your palms facing in.
- Soften your knees, then simultaneously drive your butt back as if you''re closing a door behind you while pulling your arms straight down past your hips until your hands pass by the side of your knees.
- Next, bring your arms back overhead while thrusting your hips forward until you''re standing with your arms fully extended.
- Repeat for reps, time or distance.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'flanny', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1526/view/'),
  ('e60125f0-3a88-4707-815f-fe0e2b4be3c4', 'Skipping - Standard', 'Calves', 'Do a single, double footed jump for each swing of the rope.
Work on a smooth, rhythmical movement, bouncing lightly on the balls of your feet.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Cerin', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/595/view/'),
  ('893c07ea-2e24-49b6-92e4-d0033eedec62', 'Skullcrusher Dumbbells', 'Triceps', 'Hold the dumbbells and lay down on a flat bench in such a way that around 1/4 of your head is over the edge. Stretch arms straight up and then lean dumbbells away from your toes to a 10-20 degree angle. Keep upper arm at this angle throughout exercise. Dumbbell shall not be amed at your head, but away over your head. This will maximise gain from exercise with load on triceps all the time.
Pay attention to your elbows and arms: only the triceps are doing the work, the rest of the arms should not move.', 'Bench, Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/245/view/'),
  ('95a7e546-e8f8-4521-a76b-983d94161b25', 'Skullcrusher SZ-bar', 'Triceps', 'Hold the SZ-bar and lay down on a flat bench in such a way that around 1/4 of your head is over the edge. Stretch your arms with the bar and bend them so that the bar is lowered. Just before it touches your forehead, push it up.
Pay attention to your elbows and arms: only the triceps are doing the work, the rest of the arms should not move.', 'Bench, SZ-Bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/246/view/'),
  ('965b0d80-544b-4f22-a64b-32dc033f071a', 'Skydiver with arms in T-position', null, 'Lie on your stomach with your legs more than shoulder-width apart and your feet up. Your arms are stretched out to the side and form a T with your upper body, your palms are on the floor. Now raise your upper body, arms and legs as high as you can while keeping your body under tension. Turn your thumbs towards the ceiling and pull your shoulder blades together. While keeping your upper body and all limbs in the air, bring your legs together and then spread them again, keeping them constantly in the air. For the remaining repetitions, only move your legs open and closed.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'lxmx', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1210/view/'),
  ('32cc7175-3fda-4c7e-900f-8766c22e3b2b', 'Sled Push', 'Calves, Glutes, Quads', '- Load the sled with 25% of your maximum load. If you don’t know this, choose a weight you can push for 10 minutes with short breaks. Beginners may choose to push the sled with no weight.
- Stand behind the sled and grab the poles with a high-grip hand position.
- Engage your core muscles and start pushing the sled forward as fast as you can, powering through your entire leg. Extend your hips and knees as you move the sled forward. Your foot stance should resemble your natural running position.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'flanny', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1523/view/'),
  ('90f12b1b-b33e-4d83-9f9e-e26d3a4b8e0b', 'Sleeper Stretch', 'Shoulders', '- Lie on your side so that you are resting a little weight on your shoulder blade to keep it still
- Extend your arm to 90 degrees in front of you, resting on the floor/mat
- Lift your hand with your elbow so that your fingers point to the ceiling and your palm points towards your feet
- Keeping your elbow still, gently lower your palm toward the floor', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Croak6728', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1711/view/'),
  ('55bb8725-a7c9-48a0-b192-ec6c9db6f9bc', 'Sliding Lateral Lunge', 'Hamstrings, Glutes, Quads', 'Stand upright with your feet hip-width apart, holding a kettlebell close to your chest in goblet position. Place one foot on a slider, gliding disc, or towel. Keep your chest tall and core engaged. Slowly slide the foot outward to the side while bending the opposite knee, lowering your hips into a lateral lunge. Keep the working leg’s knee aligned with the toes and avoid letting it collapse inward. The non-working leg remains straight and slides smoothly along the floor. Push through the heel of the bent leg to return to the starting position while maintaining control of the kettlebell.', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'RiccaBaro', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1604/view/'),
  ('9f9edad8-7da0-448c-bfcb-ec61f2d28162', 'Sloper hanging', null, 'Hanging on sloper holds of a fingerboard for a amount of seconds', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'marcelbader', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/804/view/'),
  ('939a8f83-efad-4556-9773-7b9410c08b3f', 'Slow Eccentric Pull-Up Negative', 'Lats', 'Stand on a box or bench under a pull-up bar and step up so your chin is above the bar with your elbows fully bent. Take your feet off the box and lower yourself as slowly as you can, aiming for five seconds, until your arms are straight. Step back onto the box and repeat. This builds the strength needed for full pull-ups.', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'lightking', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2730/view/'),
  ('2398e14f-02a6-4fdc-9f69-029e52dfe033', 'Slow Squat', 'Quads', '​A tempo-based squat that uses controlled movement to increase muscle tension and improve lower-body stability.
- ​Lower: Descend slowly for 3 seconds.
- ​Pause: Hold at the bottom for 1 second.
- ​Stand: Return to the starting position at a normal pace.
- Form: Keep your chest up and weight in your heels.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Anon#2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1963/view/'),
  ('092d0fbb-0409-470e-a087-beb9a378f3f7', 'Smith Machine Close-grip Bench Press', 'Triceps', 'Perform a standard bench press on the smith machine, but have your hands on the bar about shoulder width apart, and keep your elbows close to your body.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/598/view/'),
  ('ef2df6a2-41df-4a25-b2e0-4808dc2c3305', 'Smith Machine Slight Incline Press', 'Chest', 'Execution
Start position:
- Unrack the bar and hold it above your upper chest with arms fully extended.
- Keep your shoulder blades retracted and pressed into the bench.
Lowering phase (eccentric):
- Slowly bring the bar down to just below your collarbone or upper chest.
- Maintain control — don’t bounce the bar.
Pressing phase (concentric):
- Push the bar upward in a straight line until your arms are fully extended.
- Focus on squeezing your upper chest at the top.
Lock and repeat:
- Complete your reps, then safely rack the bar back into the hooks.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'novadani', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/925/view/'),
  ('b382dcb2-60a8-412d-b0ee-6afe9c9f36d7', 'Smith Machine Split Squat', 'Hamstrings, Quads', 'The "Smith Machine Split Squat" is a strength exercise
performed on the Smith machine, ideal for targeting the legs and glutes.
It involves placing one foot forward and the other back, lowering the
body until the front thigh is parallel to the ground while the bar
slides vertically along the machine. This exercise helps improve
stability, correct muscle imbalances, and reduces stress on the back,
providing a safe environment for strength training.', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'workout@rooven.anonaddy.me', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1593/view/'),
  ('f3ef220b-a070-449c-a463-876d30cd9b14', 'Smith machine squat', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Technique and Execution
The Smith machine squat is a variation of the traditional barbell squat that uses a guided, fixed-path barbell. Here''s a detailed breakdown of the proper technique:
Starting Position
Foot Placement: Position feet slightly forward of the bar, about shoulder-width apart
Bar Position: Rest the bar on your upper trapezius/shoulders, similar to a traditional back squat
Stance: Feet can be slightly angled outward for natural hip rotation
Squat Movement
Unrack the bar by rotating it to release from the safety hooks
Slowly lower your body by bending knees and hips
Descend until thighs are parallel to the ground (or slightly below)
Pause briefly at the bottom of the movement
Drive through your heels to return to the starting position
Benefits and Considerations
Advantages
Reduced Balance Requirements: Fixed bar path makes it easier for beginners
Controlled Movement: Less risk of improper form compared to free weight squats
Isolation: Allows focused leg muscle development', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1747/view/'),
  ('cbf4cf16-f188-4a60-a223-aa50b163f4b7', 'Smith Press', 'Shoulders', 'Sitting almost 90 degree angle, smith machine', 'Incline bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'novadani', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/916/view/'),
  ('99a7651b-afc1-4007-ab80-aac590bbfbfe', 'SMR Glutes (lax ball)', null, 'SMR Glutes (lax ball)
Part of: Limber 11
Source: Joe DeFranco
Prescription: 30 sec-2 min
Type: soft_tissue', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Pete-E automation', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1952/view/'),
  ('c8f951dd-94e0-4548-bbed-3b7bc883f7ba', 'Snap Down', 'Hamstrings, Glutes, Quads', 'Begin in a standing position with your
arms up over your head and your toes pushing into the ground raising
your heels up becoming as tall as you can. From this position, perform a
small hop with both feet slightly coming off of the ground. As you
land, bend your knees and begin to hinge forward at your hips absorbing
the landing. Return to the starting position and repeat.
You should feel the muscles in your lower body working.
Start with your arms and knees fully
straightened out. Keep a stable balance as you land and briefly hold
that end position, don’t go too fast and become off balanced. Keep your
chest up.
For a detailed video on landing mechanics, click here: https://youtu.be/RThUCYRDyZw', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'mountain.potato', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1576/view/'),
  ('8161de8b-7802-46c0-9bd4-db60f39b7677', 'Snatch', 'Shoulders', 'Stand with your feet at hip width and your shins against the bar. Grasp the bar at double shoulder width and, keeping your lower back flat, drive your heels into the floor to begin lifting the bar. When it''s above your knees, explosively extend your hips and shrug your shoulders. Let the momentum carry the weight overhead.', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC0', 'Mens Fitness', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/599/view/'),
  ('127033e0-46c5-44a6-825b-fbecfe60ed9a', 'Snatch OL', 'Shoulders, Hamstrings, Glutes, Quads', 'Olympic lift snatch
- Pull barbell from ground to overhead lockout using a wide grip and overhead squat.
- Move bar slowly until cleared knees then explosively extend hips, pull bar up and dive under the bar
- Catch bar overhead with straight arms
- Push head forward during lockout', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'M9', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1447/view/'),
  ('33a55e1a-ff83-4c34-b056-887a121b0978', 'Solo Hip Flexor Stretch', null, 'See this instructive video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qHwyBHS6MQs .', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Croak6728', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1839/view/'),
  ('b1051c3e-78a9-4b73-899c-c700098cf1a8', 'Speed Deadlift', 'Hamstrings, Glutes', 'Deadlift with short (less than one 1min) rest between sets.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'MrSteele', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/604/view/'),
  ('23d2b2ce-4ae7-441a-a122-9f6784f15aa6', 'Sphinx', 'Abs', 'Lie on your stomach flat, legs extended and the backs of your feet on
the ground. Bring your forearms under your shoulders, elbows directly
underneath. Then press your hands into the floor to lift your chest.
Draw your shoulder blades together, open your collarbones, and direct
your gaze forward, while keeping the lower back relaxed.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Joculari', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1810/view/'),
  ('b3049397-a1d1-4442-9113-3532bcd1f278', 'Spider Curl', 'Biceps', 'To get the most out of DB Spider Curls, you need an
adjustable weight bench set at a 45-degree incline. This inclined position
allows your arms to hang freely, putting them in an extended start position
with elbows slightly in front of your torso, which is the key to maintaining
low-level isometric tension throughout the movement pattern.
- Lie face-down on an adjustable
weight bench with your chest fully supported, holding a pair of dumbbells.
- Keep a neutral head position
and don’t crane your neck.
- Let your arms hang straight
down with your palms facing forward (for a standard Spider Curl) or palms
facing inward for a Hammer Curl exercise variation.
- Keep a stable foot position on
the floor to maintain balance and control.
- Start the curl motion by
flexing your biceps muscles and lifting the pair of dumbbells or curl bar
toward your shoulders.
- Keep your elbow flexion
controlled. Your arms should stay locked in position with no unnecessary
movement.
- Squeeze hard at the top for 1-2 seconds. This
is where maximum tension hits the biceps muscle fibers.
- Lower the weight under control
for 3-4 seconds to keep muscles under tension for the entire range of
motion.', 'Incline bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Moffi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1465/view/'),
  ('eaf6f39e-d46d-4460-b5a9-71e814b18f89', 'Splinter Sit-ups', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Lie on your back with your legs straight and arms at your sides, keeping your elbows bent at 90 degrees. As you sit up, twist your upper body to the right and bring your left knee toward your right elbow while you swing your left arm back. Lower your body to the starting position, and repeat to your right. That''s 1 rep.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'djblitzd', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/607/view/'),
  ('80d83f98-efe5-4d87-a403-c0feadc8650f', 'Split squats left', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Starting position:
Stand with your back straight. Take a large step forward with your left leg.
Steps:
- Bring your pelvis down until you almost touch the floor with your right knee.
- Bring your pelvis back up.
- Repeat.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/991/view/'),
  ('1d8599d8-59a5-4faf-a9cf-b81639cbf68b', 'Split squats right', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Starting position:
Stand with your back straight. Take a large step forward with your right leg.
Steps:
- Bring your pelvis down until you almost touch the floor with your left knee.
- Bring your pelvis back up.
- Repeat.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/992/view/'),
  ('4b220e25-1a42-4457-9652-274183c6d839', 'Squat -> Stand Coordination', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Custom description created by the Integrated Conditioning routine importer for wger.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2645/view/'),
  ('5c0824dc-f2fb-4d19-a1e5-7c33219ad51d', 'Squat Jumps', 'Quads', 'Jump wide, then close', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'OGhTebfCxhexZXuf35mUxV9C--A', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/614/view/'),
  ('e6890815-de67-4882-9cd5-82b7630208ad', 'Squat Sky Reach Stretch', 'Lats, Chest', '- Drop into a squat.
- Hold one ankle with the opposite hand.
- Fold your other arm to your chest.
- Staying in the squat and keeping hold of your ankle, twist to turn your chest-hand back/outwards until your elbow is pointing at the sky.
- Return to the neutral squatting position with your hand on your chest. Repeat movement as required.
Repeat on both sides.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Croak6728', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2528/view/'),
  ('30ac081b-fb79-4253-9457-8efc07568790', 'Squat Thrust', 'Glutes, Quads, Abs, Soleus', 'The burpee, or squat thrust, is a full body exercise used in strength training and as an aerobic exercise. The basic movement is performed in four steps and known as a four-count burpee: Begin in a standing position. Move into a squat position with your hands on the ground. (count 1) Kick your feet back into a plank position, while keeping your arms extended. (count 2) Immediately return your feet into squat position. (count 3) Stand up from the squat position (count 4)', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC0', 'BFad07', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/616/view/'),
  ('a2f5b6ef-b780-49c0-8d96-fdaff23e27ce', 'Squats', 'Quads', 'Place a barbell in a rack just below shoulder-height. Dip under the bar to put it behind the neck across the top of the back, and grip the bar with the hands wider than shoulder-width apart. Lift the chest up and squeeze the shoulder blades together to keep the straight back throughout the entire movement. Stand up to bring the bar off the rack and step backwards, then place the feet so that they are a little wider than shoulder-width apart. Sit back into hips and keep the back straight and the chest up, squatting down so the hips are below the knees. From the bottom of the squat, press feet into the ground and push hips forward to return to the top of the standing position.', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/615/view/'),
  ('316aca47-6f5b-40b7-b04f-f69e68387354', 'Squats on Multipress', 'Quads', null, null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/341/view/'),
  ('b3e433ed-0247-46b3-be77-9f2738a09a48', 'Stair Master', 'Hamstrings, Calves, Glutes, Quads', 'cardio and lower-body strength machine designed to simulate climbing stairs. It provides a low-impact, high-intensity workout that targets the legs, glutes, and core while improving cardiovascular endurance.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'MisterPinnacle', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1548/view/'),
  ('93b9b84c-7b72-4712-a7c2-0e14a5fab32a', 'Standing Adduction (Cable)', null, 'Keep your hips square and still, pulling the cable across your opposing leg using only your inner thigh.
Don’t let your torso lean or twist — no side bending to help the rep.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'barry', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1724/view/'),
  ('eb61c7a1-e1c9-4c44-a8ce-2bbe98a39857', 'Standing Bicep Curl', 'Biceps, Brachialis', 'Stand holding dumbbells at shoulder width apart. Face forearm upward and keep upper arm still while raising each dumbbell up to your shoulder.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC0', 'BFad07', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/621/view/'),
  ('a569e8c3-218f-40db-9d71-e155c68cb26e', 'Standing biceps stretch left', 'Shoulders, Biceps, Chest', 'Stand with your left arm close to a wall. Extend your left arm and put your left hand on the wall, then gently turn your body to the right.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Lynn_McIntyre', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1232/view/'),
  ('671568f9-6653-4e11-ac71-3f34c74329a0', 'Standing biceps stretch right', 'Shoulders, Biceps, Chest', 'Stand with your right arm close to a wall. Extend your right arm and put your right hand on the wall, then gently turn your body to the left.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Lynn_McIntyre', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1233/view/'),
  ('7ce443b6-eb84-4f65-b05f-461c1cc8bcc0', 'Standing Calf Raises', 'Calves', 'Get onto the calf raises machine, you should able to completely push your calves down. Stand straight, don''t make a hollow back and don''t bend your legs. Pull yourself up as high as you can. Make a small pause of 1 - 2 seconds and go slowly down.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/622/view/'),
  ('f36dfa40-7586-4721-a434-1fd878232c62', 'Standing Calf Stretch', 'Calves', 'This stretch targets the gastrocnemius (the chief muscle of the calf of the leg, which flexes the knee and foot). It is easy to perform anywhere. All you need is a wall or a chair.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'erikocobra', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1239/view/'),
  ('083bc8a6-0eb4-4049-b2ef-88415aa15459', 'Standing Dowel Shoulder press', null, 'Mobility - prone dowel press or standing dowel shoulder press alternating between front andback', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'mountain.potato', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1575/view/'),
  ('f4bb000a-c855-4497-95e0-abc1b71725e7', 'Standing IT Band Stretch', null, 'View the video to undestand the exercise', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Davidgj32', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1875/view/'),
  ('57e4fee7-0213-4005-be1f-178400505ea1', 'Standing Leg Lift with External Rotation (Custom)', 'Glutes', 'Stand and lift one bent knee out to the side with external rotation, move slowly under control, ten reps per side, hold a wall for balance if needed, keep the torso upright.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ruochenma', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2758/view/'),
  ('af59f161-147a-49ba-85c8-6c9f4e5ffe63', 'Standing Pancake', 'Hamstrings', 'Stand and open your legs as wide as possible into a straddle position. Then bend at the hips, pushing them back while keeping your back straight.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1396/view/'),
  ('aa3c2791-74f7-4fed-a812-297ff454e2f9', 'Standing Pancake Good Morning', 'Hamstrings', 'Stand with your feet wider than shoulder-width apart and toes pointed slightly outward. Place your hands on the back of your head. Hinge at your hips and lower your upper body towards the ground, keeping your back straight and chest open.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1397/view/'),
  ('7dc59fa0-5552-4ffa-954f-9bc8c22740bf', 'Standing Rope Forearm', null, 'Grab a wrist roller tool with both hands while standing with your feet about shoulder width apart. If your gym does not have a wrist roller tool, you can easily put one together. All you need is a 5 or 10 pound weight plate, a strong thin rope about 3 feet long and a 6-8 inch stick or bar. Securely fasten the rope to the middle of the bar/stick and tie the other end of the rope to the weight plate. To begin this exercise, grab the bar/stick with both hands using an overhand grip. Extend both arms straight out in front of you, parallel to the floor. Next, roll the weight up from the floor by rapidly twisting the bar/stick with your hands and wrists. Once the weight reaches the top, slowly lower the plate back to the floor by reversing the motion of your hands and wrists. Repeat (if you can!).', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'pwiltrout', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/623/view/'),
  ('864b0a80-5d29-4d87-9144-f6ee6f5087b9', 'Standing Side Crunches', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Standing side crunches are a dynamic exercise that targets the obliques while improving balance and stability. Stand upright with your feet shoulder-width apart and hands behind your head or holding a dumbbell in one hand. Lean your torso to one side, contracting your obliques, while bringing your elbow toward your hip (or crunching toward the weight if using a dumbbell). Return to the starting position and alternate sides or perform all repetitions on one side before switching.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'hpmbala@gmail.com', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1426/view/'),
  ('56631e7a-471a-469c-958a-c3ff78b1e73c', 'Standing Soleus Stretch', 'Soleus', 'This stretch targets the Soleus part of your calf. It may be performed with a wall or chair.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'erikocobra', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1240/view/'),
  ('4d5decb3-5fbe-45c1-9ad5-ff715e53cb34', 'Stationary Bike', null, 'Ride a Stationary Bike with various tensions.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'intelman', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/624/view/'),
  ('5da3d393-0c4e-4ac8-96b4-aab51f6ab3d1', 'Stationary bike cardio', null, 'Benefits of Stationary Bike Cardio
Stationary bike cardio is an excellent form of exercise that offers multiple benefits for health and well-being. Here are some of the most notable ones:
Improved Cardiovascular Health
- Strengthens the heart: Regular exercise helps improve the heart''s efficiency and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.
- Increases circulation: It improves blood flow, which can help lower blood pressure.
Calorie Burning and Weight Loss
- Efficient for burning calories: Depending on the intensity, you can burn between 400 and 600 calories per hour.
- Helps with weight loss: Combined with a balanced diet, stationary bike cardio can be effective for losing weight.
Muscle Strengthening
- Tones the legs: It mainly works the leg muscles, including the quadriceps, hamstrings and calves.
- Improves muscular endurance: Over time, you can increase the endurance and strength in your legs.
Mental Health Benefits
- Reduces stress: Exercise releases endorphins, which can improve your mood and reduce stress.
- Boosts energy: Regular physical activity can raise your energy levels and improve sleep quality.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1618/view/'),
  ('09235a96-9834-4fad-ba9a-88ace035e80a', 'Step Calf Stretch', 'Calves', 'Stand on a step or a sturdy raised surface with the balls of both feet on the edge and the heels hanging off, holding a rail or wall for balance. Let one or both heels drop below the level of the step until you feel a stretch through the calf, and hold. Keep the knee straight to stretch the gastrocnemius, or bend the knee slightly to bias the soleus lower down. Hold for the prescribed time, typically 30 seconds, without bouncing, then switch sides if stretching one leg at a time.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'trs1800', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2666/view/'),
  ('2f10d91f-6c12-471b-bb9e-80840a56ce01', 'Step Jack', 'Quads', 'The Step Jack is a low-impact plyometric-style move that provides cardiovascular benefits without the joint stress of jumping. It works the entire body, specifically targeting the legs, core, and shoulders. This move is ideal for beginners, individuals with joint sensitivities, or as a dynamic warm-up to gradually elevate the heart rate.
Notes (Instructions):
- ​Stand upright with your feet together and arms at your sides.
- ​Step your right foot out to the side while simultaneously swinging both arms up above your head.
- Bring your right foot back to the center and lower your arms.
- ​Immediately repeat the movement, stepping out with your left foot.
- ​Continue alternating sides at a steady, rhythmic pace.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Anon#2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1962/view/'),
  ('9e6dae29-5d03-440a-bdf1-2cb25a5179c2', 'Step-ups', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Starting position:
Stand facing a chair.
Steps:
- Step up onto the chair.
- Step off the chair.
- Repeat.', 'Bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/981/view/'),
  ('22c59ede-970b-43e1-bb51-ac1c2be0b0e0', 'Stiff-legged Deadlifts', 'Hamstrings', '- Keep legs straight
- Keep back straight', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/627/view/'),
  ('9776bcb9-90a8-4f20-898f-652f82cabd5f', 'Stomach Vacuum', 'Abs', '- Starting position: Lie flat on your back with knees bent, feet flat on the floor (or stand/sit upright).
- Exhalation: Fully exhale all the air from your lungs through your mouth.
- Contraction: Draw your navel in towards your spine as far as possible, pulling your stomach inward and up under your ribcage.
- Hold: Hold this isometric contraction without breathing for 10 to 20 seconds.
- Release: Slowly inhale through your nose to release the tension and relax your abdominals.
- Repeat: Rest briefly and perform the desired number of sets.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'TimoteoG', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2673/view/'),
  ('d2cf769a-9271-41ae-90fc-aef813e41740', 'Straddle L-Sit', 'Abs', 'With your legs in a sitting saddle position, push your body upwards off the ground. Your legs should be horizontal and point straight outwards. Your arms should be between your legs.
Hold isometrically as long as required.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Croak6728', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1847/view/'),
  ('36274d27-3812-4b8c-80e2-ab59acf82c4d', 'Straight Bar Cable Curls', 'Biceps', 'Standing upright in front of Cable Tower using a straight bar', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'novadani', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/912/view/'),
  ('ca16aa3c-9b43-4d81-ae60-1e225163b767', 'Straight Bar Cable Front Raise', 'Shoulders', 'Back to cable tower, cable between legs, SZ Bar', 'Cable machine, SZ-Bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'novadani', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/917/view/'),
  ('e7b0bc52-a9c3-452b-887e-e80523506b9b', 'Straight Leg Hamstring Stretch', 'Hamstrings', 'Lie on your back with both legs extended. Raise one leg straight up and gently pull it towards your upper body with both hands around your thigh. Keep the knee as straight as possible and your lower back on the mat. Hold the stretch, then switch legs.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'SMS', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2761/view/'),
  ('0f5fc602-afb6-4500-87da-f115f3ef3f47', 'Straight-arm Pull Down (bar Attachment)', 'Lats', 'Use the straight bar attachment on a high pulley. Grasp the two ends of the bar with your palms facing downward and your arms straight out in front of you. Pull your hands down towards your hips, while keeping your arms straight, then raise them back up to the starting position.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/628/view/'),
  ('7c4c64ca-6948-4ae4-a0e0-8c56a4699dff', 'Straight-arm Pull Down (bar Attachment) (Custom)', 'Lats', 'Perform Straight-arm Pull Down (bar Attachment) with controlled form, keeping tension on the target muscles, pausing briefly at the peak contraction, then returning slowly under control without using momentum.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ruochenma', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2712/view/'),
  ('2f7149c3-77ce-4313-a59c-aef82b5a730a', 'Straight-arm Pull Down (rope Attachment)', 'Lats', 'Use the rope attachment on a high pulley. Grasp the two ends of the rope with your arms straight out in front of you. Pull your hands down towards your hips, while keeping your arms straight, then raise them back up to the starting position.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/629/view/'),
  ('a45123c6-34c2-43cf-b6bf-914a8f40995a', 'Straight-Arm Pulldown (Cable)', 'Lats', 'Keep arms straight but not locked, pulling with your lats, not your shoulders.
Don’t let your shoulders shrug up during the movement.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'barry', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1726/view/'),
  ('1c17c370-dd74-46c4-b4f2-039c510af058', 'Stroke-and-Roll Drill', 'Shoulders, Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Push off, swim a few strokes, and every breath roll fully onto your back for 2-3 breaths, then roll back into freestyle. This teaches you to rotate your body as a unit rather than just turning your head, and helps remove the fear of not having your face in the water.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Imported from swim plan', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2485/view/'),
  ('b6c19c36-34be-4d9f-8c07-8bdf9ab3ba1a', 'Suitcase Carry', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Walk forward and backward with holding dumbbell on one side without leaning to counter the weight.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'aboksz', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1776/view/'),
  ('0c2d9b74-3d1e-481f-a7bf-3b3532f7d6b0', 'Sumo Deadlift', 'Hamstrings, Glutes, Quads', '- Begin with a bar loaded on the ground. Approach the bar so that the bar intersects the middle of the feet. The feet should be set very wide, near the collars. Bend at the hips to grip the bar. The arms should be directly below the shoulders, inside the legs, and you can use a pronated grip, a mixed grip, or hook grip. Relax the shoulders, which in effect lengthens your arms.
- Take a breath, and then lower your hips, looking forward with your head with your chest up. Drive through the floor, spreading your feet apart, with your weight on the back half of your feet. Extend through the hips and knees.
- As the bar passes through the knees, lean back and drive the hips into the bar, pulling your shoulder blades together.
- Return the weight to the ground by bending at the hips and controlling the weight on the way down.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'magdy', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/630/view/'),
  ('0a5a7661-1e24-4c15-bb2b-503672141307', 'Sumo Squats', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Stand with your feet wider than your shoulders, with your toes pointed out at a 45 degree angle and barbell on your shoulder.
While keeping your back straight, descend slowly by bending at the knees and hips as if you are sitting down (squatting).
Lower yourself until your quadriceps and hamstrings are parallel to the floor.
Return to the starting position by pressing upwards and extending your legs while maintaining an equal distribution of weight on your forefoot and heel.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'sophialj', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/632/view/'),
  ('66c90ca7-23d8-47b1-820f-dc0c8140603b', 'Superman', 'Glutes, Lats', 'Lay flat on your stomach with your arms extended in front of you on the ground as your legs are lying flat. Lift both your arms and legs at the same time, as if you were flying, and contract the lower back. Make sure that you are breathing and, depending on your fitness level, hold the movement for at least two to five seconds per repetition.', 'Gym mat', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'baldurmen', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/636/view/'),
  ('2fe15ae4-085b-4116-8d73-2a120cc2a231', 'Supine Hip Abduction', 'Glutes, Quads', '- Starting Position:
Lie on your back (supine) on a mat, with your legs extended and
relaxed. Arms can be placed alongside your body or out to the sides for
stability.
- The Movement:
Slightly lift one leg off the floor (keeping it straight or with a
slight bend). Move it laterally outward, away from the other leg, as if
stepping over a small obstacle.
- Range of Motion:
Move the leg as far as comfortable without lifting your pelvis or
rotating your torso, feeling a stretch in the inner thigh and hip.
- Return: Slowly bring the leg back to the starting position with controlled movement.
- Goal: Improves lateral hip mobility (abduction) and strengthens the pelvic stabilizer muscles.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'teus_ergaster', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1886/view/'),
  ('2c31daac-17b1-4cc9-b1c1-f34266ee2038', 'Supine press', 'Trapezius', 'Take a close grip and push perpendicular to the bench. Keep back flat.', 'Barbell, Bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'CptnFatbeard', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1583/view/'),
  ('86237d20-f4e3-49a6-bf28-9e4660377bc9', 'Supino inclinado', 'Chest', 'Inclinado 90 graus e movimentos leves e precisos', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1778/view/'),
  ('b81f4e3c-64e2-4fbd-9198-d422f6f2151f', 'Supported Calf Stretch', null, 'View the video to undestand the exercise', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Davidgj32', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1876/view/'),
  ('f9e06649-21ec-47a0-b73c-0a79f046f1c7', 'Supported Deep Squat Hold', 'Glutes, Quads', 'Hold a stable support in front of you and lower into a comfortable deep squat with both feet grounded. Use the support for balance and to control depth. Maintain the squat hold without bouncing or forcing the hips or ankles farther than a comfortable range.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Original exercise instructions prepared for nalhilal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2696/view/'),
  ('15cfef4a-471f-4a00-a3c3-973b15bd7cda', 'Supported Split Squat', 'Quads', 'Stand in a split stance with one foot about a stride in front of the other, and hold on to a stable support such as a rack, a wall or the back of a chair.
Lower your hips straight down by bending both knees until the back knee almost touches the floor. Keep your upper body upright and the front heel on the floor, then push through the front foot back up. Use the support only for balance, not to pull yourself up. Complete all repetitions, then switch sides.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2557/view/'),
  ('e0d9e9ef-09ee-4d26-9504-622093810414', 'Suspended crossess', 'Chest', 'Suspension exercise with trx for chest training', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/927/view/'),
  ('6c8b0c67-68af-4473-907e-31fb1e48430c', 'Swimming 50m sprints', null, '50m swimming sprints at 1min', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'abuono', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/961/view/'),
  ('1eeccede-29c5-4f38-9ba7-d77c7c47993d', 'T-Bar row', 'Lats, Trapezius', 'bent over with triangle grip, slightly bent knees', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'novadani', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/919/view/'),
  ('42657bd4-bf4e-4429-8e6a-2f5f585aa22a', 'Talons fesses', 'Calves', 'Touch your heels to your buttocks, while remaining static or moving', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'painDpice', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1285/view/'),
  ('51420ded-5c17-4ce0-b005-89f1b67b7c65', 'Thruster', 'Shoulders', '- Start by doing a front squat
- At the top position, push the bar above your head (similar to a press)
- Lower the bar to the shoulders', 'Barbell', 'wger', 'CC0', 'BeLikeWater', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/650/view/'),
  ('f5252fbe-1e00-4b8d-8781-2e56d6a5250f', 'Tibialis raises', null, '- Stand up straight
- Lift the balls of your feet, resting on your heels.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1200/view/'),
  ('33728393-2b84-4d39-8b68-462222e004e3', 'Toe Taps', 'Abs', 'Toe taps are a core-focused exercise performed lying on your back. Lie flat with your arms by your sides and your legs raised to a tabletop position (knees bent at 90 degrees). Slowly lower one foot to gently tap the floor, keeping your core engaged and your lower back pressed into the ground. Return to the starting position and alternate legs. This exercise strengthens the core while minimizing strain on the lower back.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'hpmbala@gmail.com', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1425/view/'),
  ('a51de8df-4101-45cb-8da5-de230d9bac1d', 'Toe Touch', 'Hamstrings, Glutes', 'Stand with your feet closer together. Hinge at your hips and lower your upper body towards your toes. Reach your hands towards your feet, try to touch your toes, the ground, or as far down your legs as you can comfortably go.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'captive0592', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1390/view/'),
  ('42d09639-a5a6-4aa6-b115-e4c96b1d3735', 'Toes to bar', 'Abs', 'The name "Toes to Bar" says it all: This exercise, in which you hang from a pull-up bar, involves bringing your toes toward the bar, pointing toward your face. This really works your abdominal muscles. "Toes to Bar" is popular in functional fitness programs like Freeletics and CrossFit, and is an effective exercise for six-pack training.', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'florian.bussmann', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1529/view/'),
  ('1e9b886f-0dbe-4434-829e-7159bcc40692', 'Torso rotation stretch', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'The torso rotation stretch helps improve spinal mobility and relieves tension in the lower back and obliques. It can be performed standing or seated.
- Standing Variation: Stand upright with your feet shoulder-width apart. Place your hands on your hips or extend your arms in front of you. Slowly rotate your torso to one side, keeping your hips stable. Hold for a few seconds, then rotate to the opposite side.
- Seated Variation: Sit on a chair with your feet flat on the floor. Place one hand on the outside of your opposite thigh and gently twist your torso toward that side. Hold the stretch before switching sides.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'hpmbala@gmail.com', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1451/view/'),
  ('56a03260-2901-4b28-bd41-a030185f985a', 'Torso Twist', 'Abs', 'Standing Torso Twist WorkoutTarget your core and improve flexibility with this dynamic standing exercise! The Standing Torso Twist Workout involves twisting your torso while keeping your feet and hips stable, engaging your obliques, and stretching your entire upper body. This movement helps to:Strengthen core musclesIncrease flexibility in the spine and torsoImprove posture and balanceEnhance overall athletic performancePerform 3 sets of 10-15 reps, twisting to each side, to feel the benefits of this effective and efficient workout!', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'brucem', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1377/view/'),
  ('c4809dbc-0432-447d-8bb5-fa20a16b7559', 'Towel Grip Dead Hang', null, 'Hang two towels over a pull-up bar, one for each hand. Grip a towel tightly in each hand and hang with your arms straight and your shoulders active for as long as you can. The thick, unstable towels make your grip work much harder than a normal bar. This builds crushing grip strength for arm wrestling.', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'lightking', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2732/view/'),
  ('ce0c876f-e44b-48e2-8d76-c2bbb15f9872', 'Towel Pull-Up', 'Lats', 'Hang two towels over a pull-up bar and grip one in each hand. Pull yourself up until your hands are near your chin, then lower slowly to straight arms. Holding the towels makes your grip work much harder than a normal pull-up. Use assistance or do negatives if you cannot do full reps yet.', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'lightking', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2746/view/'),
  ('f44cf553-067e-4f3a-b35b-e16549c1d055', 'Towel Superman', 'Lats', 'In this exercise, you lie flat on your stomach, hold a
towel with your arms extended in front of you, and pull it apart to
create tension. Then, you move it forward and back under your stomach.
This exercise targets the back and shoulder muscles as well as core
stability.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Yderkone', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1455/view/'),
  ('d18407c2-75d0-43d8-8bbc-dfbb256b6b2f', 'Trap Bar Squat', 'Quads', 'A combination of squat and deadlift principles with the use of a hex/trap bar, easing stress on the lower back by better centering the weight.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'kmcalderwood', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1803/view/'),
  ('41b9f5a5-73de-414d-b08a-54025eccedc6', 'Trap press', 'Trapezius', 'Laying flat with arm pointing straight upwards, elbow fully extended, use only your shoulder to raise the weight as high as comfortable and back to rest.', 'Barbell, Bench, Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'CptnFatbeard', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1581/view/'),
  ('62f90b5c-7451-4fd0-91ae-4dd93c51aa41', 'Trap-3 Raise', 'Trapezius', '- Hinge forwards and bend your knees slightly. Depress and retract the shoulder you will use. Rest the other hand on your knee.
- Raise your hand about 15 degrees overhead, maintaining the position of your shoulder.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Croak6728', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1834/view/'),
  ('3f8e5871-4895-48c9-928d-dc1bc134f57b', 'Treadmill Cardio', null, 'It''s a cardiovascular workout performed on a treadmill to improve endurance, burn calories, and promote heart health. It offers multiple benefits, such as the ability to easily adjust speed and incline, allowing for a variety of workouts, from hill walking to high-intensity interval training (HIIT) and tempo runs. To optimize results, it''s important to vary your workouts.
Benefits of Treadmill Cardio:
- Improved Cardiovascular Health:
Regular aerobic exercise on a treadmill strengthens the heart and lungs, which can help prevent heart disease.
- Weight Loss:
By burning calories, treadmill training can be an effective tool for weight loss and maintaining a healthy weight.
- Muscle Strengthening:
It strengthens the muscles in the legs and glutes, helping to prevent muscle loss, which is especially important with age.
- Training Flexibility:
Allows you to vary speed, incline, and intervals, making your workout more dynamic and adaptable to different fitness levels.
Types of Treadmill Training:
- Hill Walking:
Increasing the incline of the treadmill and walking at a brisk pace can improve endurance and work your leg muscles.
- Endurance Jog:
A run at a comfortable, steady pace that keeps your heart rate in a target zone (such as Zone 2) to build endurance.
- Interval Training:
Alternating periods of high intensity (fast running) with periods of low intensity (easy walking or jogging) to maximize calorie burn and cardiovascular improvement.
- Tempo Run:
A run at a steady, sustained pace that is slightly faster than a normal jog, which challenges your ability to sustain a more intense effort for an extended period.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Tierrasverdes', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1615/view/'),
  ('5915fabe-c941-4dac-b196-bc4e8c7ce57b', 'Tricep Dumbbell Kickback', 'Triceps', '- Start with a dumbbell in each hand and your palms facing your torso. Keep your back straight with a slight bend in the knees and bend forward at the waist. Your torso should be almost parallel to the floor. Make sure to keep your head up. Your upper arms should be close to your torso and parallel to the floor. Your forearms should be pointed towards the floor as you hold the weights. There should be a 90-degree angle formed between your forearm and upper arm. This is your starting position.
- Now, while keeping your upper arms stationary, exhale and use your triceps to lift the weights until the arm is fully extended. Focus on moving the forearm.
- After a brief pause at the top contraction, inhale and slowly lower the dumbbells back down to the starting position.
- Repeat the movement for the prescribed amount of repetitions.
Variations: This exercise can be executed also one arm at a time much like the one arm rows are performed.
Also, if you like the one arm variety, you can use a low pulley handle instead of a dumbbell for better peak contraction. In this case, the palms should be facing up (supinated grip) as opposed to the torso (neutral grip).', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'http://www.bodybuilding.com/', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/655/view/'),
  ('ea63d85c-8579-4dda-b99f-c4c8930f9af6', 'Tricep Pushdown on Cable', 'Triceps', 'The cable rope push-down is a popular exercise targeting the triceps muscles. It''s easy to learn and perform, making it a favorite for everyone from beginners to advanced lifters. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more per set, as part of an upper-body or arm-focused workout.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cshep442', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/805/view/'),
  ('fc608f0e-d754-4911-ae59-8856952e7064', 'Tricep Rope Pushdowns', 'Triceps', '- Setup: Stand facing the cable, feet shoulder-width apart, with a slight forward lean.
- Grip: Hold the rope with palms facing each other and elbows tucked tight to your ribs.
- Execution: Push the rope down until arms are fully straight, pulling the ends apart at the bottom.
- Control: Keep upper arms still and return to the start with a slow, controlled motion.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'nishant0712', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1900/view/'),
  ('3dd0db1c-450a-46d1-ad46-d7854bdc26ed', 'Triceps Dips (Assisted)', 'Triceps', 'Assisted triceps dips  is a gym work out exercise that targets triceps and also involves chest.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'matpn', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1372/view/'),
  ('3188868c-84e2-4a3b-b7dc-f79d8650988d', 'Triceps Extensions on Cable', 'Triceps', 'Grab the cable, stand with your feet shoulder wide, keep your back straight and lean forward a little. Push the bar down, making sure the elbows don''t move during the exercise. Rotate your hands outwards at the very end and go back to the initial position without pause.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/659/view/'),
  ('f00630d6-578f-487f-8bf5-39c96366ccb8', 'Triceps Extensions on Cable With Bar', 'Triceps', 'Grab the bar, stand with your feet shoulder wide, keep your back straight and lean forward a little. Push the bar down, making sure the elbows don''t move during the exercise. Without pause go back to the initial position.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/660/view/'),
  ('6c671e43-497f-4863-8d78-b592f3b5e7c6', 'Triceps on Machine', 'Triceps', 'Sit down and hold the bar firmly with your hands. Now press the weight upwards (don''t fully extend your arms) and lower it slowly again. As with other triceps exercises, it''s important not to move your upper arms.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/661/view/'),
  ('19133a0d-e6b2-4d57-8a84-8aac3d962a99', 'Triceps Overhead (Dumbbell)', 'Triceps', '- Keep the elbows fixed
- Engage the muscles
- Don''t use momentum
- Controlled movement
- Slow movement
- Don''t overextend', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Anastasious', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1336/view/'),
  ('f252e9cf-3749-413d-a18e-704c6bf1b87d', 'Triceps push up', 'Triceps', 'Push ups for targeting triceps.
- Start with a plank position with your hands directly below your shoulders, feet together.
- When descending, keep your elbows pinned to your sides. Upper arms straight back.
- Lower until your chest touches the floor, then return.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'JuustoKakku', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2684/view/'),
  ('6ebb138e-bb0a-402e-84e5-68fe0896e897', 'Triceps Pushdown', 'Triceps', 'Triceps pushdown on cable using lat bar.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'anto.kreegyr', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1185/view/'),
  ('0864893a-5189-44db-8196-d64db68c9c44', 'Triceps Pushdown (Custom)', 'Triceps', 'Perform Triceps Pushdown with controlled form, keeping tension on the target muscles, pausing briefly at the peak contraction, then returning slowly under control without using momentum.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ruochenma', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2716/view/'),
  ('788b66aa-7e59-493f-ad9a-0522736c7ef0', 'Triceps Pushdown Isometric', 'Triceps', 'A single-arm isometric or isolated triceps pushdown is an isolation cable exercise where you pin your upper arm against your torso, hinge at the hips, and extend the elbow to achieve a peak contraction in the triceps muscle.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'HexoctusEngineering', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2541/view/'),
  ('aa72741d-17ae-410e-b49a-1df7aaf0afe1', 'Triceps stretch left', 'Triceps', 'Put your left hand on your back, use your right hand to grab your left elbow and gently pull it. Hold this position for a few seconds.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Lynn_McIntyre', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1230/view/'),
  ('1b9968f4-a00b-4157-8847-1c3c7dbb0d5a', 'Triceps stretch right', 'Triceps', 'Put your right hand on your back, use your left hand to grab your right elbow and gently pull it. Hold this position for a few seconds.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Lynn_McIntyre', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1231/view/'),
  ('90e9f590-7d68-4afb-9f95-8429246ea4aa', 'Trunk Rotation With Cable', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Seated trunk rotation with cable', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Robertcoop', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/672/view/'),
  ('8019be2c-47a3-475f-8e16-a2fca761a8fe', 'TRX dips', 'Triceps', 'Start with the hands on the suspension trainer and feet on the ground.
Your hands should be under your shoulders with the feet slightly in front.
Lower yourself by bringing the hips between the handles and flexing the elbows.
Extend the elbows to come back to the starting position and repeat.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1269/view/'),
  ('f9329ed4-8185-49a6-a0bb-e0f21c6cc3d8', 'TRX gorilla biceps curl', 'Brachialis', 'We''ll stand facing the TRX straps and grab them with our fists facing each other, facing forward, with our arms fully extended at about shoulder-width apart. We''ll keep our body straight, backward, as in the previous exercises, since we''ll be lifting our body toward the TRX straps by activating our biceps. Our body will be straight, with our feet flat on the floor. This time, we''ll place our arms open to the sides and at chest height. The movement we''ll perform is a contraction toward our chest. To achieve this, we''ll bend our elbows so that, by activating our biceps, we can pull our body toward the straps.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1261/view/'),
  ('f36a9fd0-7bd2-42a4-85fa-902b7cf3be45', 'TRX hammer curl', 'Biceps', 'Place the grips as if they were a continuation of the straps. We will position our arms stretched forward and our body leaning back, completely straight. The biceps muscles will activate as you bend your elbows to bring your body towards the straps. The contraction movement will end with a slight outward movement of the hands, due to the grip we are using.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1260/view/'),
  ('6679b2ad-91b7-4a75-9230-3ae1101bdd50', 'TRX Obliques', 'Obliquus externus abdominis, Abs, Serratus anterior', '- Place your feet in the stirrups and assume a high plank with your hands directly beneath your shoulders.
- Pull your knees to your right elbow, then push them back out and to the centre.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1259/view/'),
  ('069711c7-565a-4abb-90a7-614f66dd9dc3', 'TRX roll out', 'Abs', 'Hold the handles in your hands, extend your arms and keep them parallel at chest height. The movement you need to perform is to open your arms in line with your shoulders while leaning your body forward. Try not to extend your shoulders beyond 90°, and keep your abdominal muscles and glutes contracted. Muscles involved: transverse abdominis, rectus abdominis and spinal erectors.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1246/view/'),
  ('e2599e86-d8b0-434e-8a48-aa3c2df7e790', 'TRX Rows', 'Shoulders, Biceps, Lats', 'This exercise serves as a lead-up to Pull Ups.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/959/view/'),
  ('7f2628bf-6381-4228-9840-b75cbcc843a1', 'Trx Single Arm Bicep Curl', 'Biceps, Brachialis', 'Step 1: Stand comfortably and grasp the handles of the suspension system. Step 2: Lean back resting your weight on one arm, keepng the spine neutral. Step 3: Slowly curl to pull your body weight to the up position. Supinate the arm and squeeze as you approach the end position. Step 4: Lower yourself back down and repeat.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1262/view/'),
  ('de8a3db6-2b05-4c4e-977b-b61342718356', 'TRX Tricep Extension', 'Triceps', 'Attach the TRX or other suspension system overhead, having the handles hanging at chest level or slightly lower. Experiment to see what height allows you to set up in the best way.
Stand in front of the handles and grab them with your palms facing down.
Engage your abs, squeeze your glutes, take a breath and lean forward, lifting your heels off the floor. Keep your elbows straight.
Take another breath and lower yourself slowly by bending your elbows.
Go down until your elbows are at a 90-degree angle (to the point where your wrists are above your elbows). Hold the position for a moment.
Extend your arms by engaging your triceps and bring yourself to the starting position as you exhale.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cynomops', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1266/view/'),
  ('bb359e6f-a18c-460d-8572-b99b8b0abee7', 'TRX/Rings Chest Fly', 'Chest', '- Set TRX straps or rings at about mid-chest height. Stand facing away from the anchor.
- Hold one handle or ring in each hand, palms facing each other. Step forward into a lean so the straps are taut, arms nearly straight in front of the chest with a slight bend in the elbows.
- Brace the core and glutes so the body forms one straight line from head to heels. Keep the shoulders down and away from the ears.
- Keeping the same elbow bend, open the arms out to the sides in a wide arc and let the body lower forward.
- Stop when the hands are roughly level with the shoulders and you feel a stretch across the chest. Do not let the hands travel behind the body line.
- Squeeze the chest to bring the arms back together in front of you, raising the body to the start. Exhale as the hands meet, pause briefly, and repeat.
Difficulty is set by foot position: feet closer to the anchor and a steeper lean make the movement harder; a more upright stance makes it easier. The same pattern works on a suspension trainer or gymnastics rings.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tuna2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2763/view/'),
  ('4a8f0f11-1151-4e68-a872-0a8dd95f4b8b', 'TRX/Rings Dip', 'Chest, Triceps', '- Set TRX straps or rings so the handles hang at a height that lets you support your body with straight arms and the feet clear of the floor, or lightly assisted if needed.
- Grip one handle or ring in each hand. Jump or step into a support hold: arms locked out, shoulders packed down, chest up, and the body tight from head to heels. On rings, turn the palms slightly inward (rings turned out) for a stable lockout.
- Brace the core and lean the torso slightly forward.
- Bend the elbows and lower under control until the upper arms are about parallel to the floor, or to a depth you can reverse without shoulder pain. Keep the elbows close to the body rather than flaring wide.
- On rings, keep the rings close to the sides so they do not drift outward.
- Press through the hands to extend the arms and return to the support hold. Exhale on the way up, pause briefly at the top with full lockout, and repeat.
Assisted variations: keep the feet on the floor or a box and use the legs lightly. Harder variations: slower eccentrics, pauses at the bottom, or a more forward lean for more chest emphasis.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tuna2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2767/view/'),
  ('5c7887cc-cedb-4422-ad1c-e08496bf5381', 'TRX/Rings Face Pull', 'Trapezius', '- Set TRX straps or rings at about head to mid-chest height. Stand facing the anchor and hold one handle or ring in each hand with a neutral grip.
- Walk the feet toward the anchor and lean back until the arms are extended in front of you and the body forms a straight line from head to heels.
- Brace the core and glutes. Soften the knees slightly and keep the ribs down.
- Pull the handles or rings toward the face by driving the elbows high and wide, roughly to ear height. Externally rotate the shoulders so the hands finish near the temples or just outside the ears, thumbs pointing back if the straps allow.
- Squeeze the shoulder blades together at the end of the pull without shrugging toward the ears. Pause briefly.
- Extend the arms under control back to the start and repeat.
Keep constant tension in the straps. A steeper lean increases difficulty; a more upright stance makes it easier.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tuna2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2766/view/'),
  ('3616d2a5-91da-49b0-9ff1-db74274abacd', 'TRX/Rings Push-Up', 'Chest', '- Set TRX straps or rings so the handles hang just above the floor, or at a height that allows a full push-up range with your feet on the ground.
- Grip one handle or ring in each hand. Get into a plank with the body in one straight line from head to heels, arms extended, and the straps vertical under the shoulders.
- Brace the core and glutes. Keep the wrists neutral and the shoulders packed down away from the ears.
- Bend the elbows and lower the chest between the hands until the upper arms are about parallel to the floor or you reach a comfortable depth.
- Keep the elbows at roughly 45 degrees from the torso; do not flare them fully out to the sides.
- Press through the hands to extend the arms and return to the plank. Exhale on the way up, pause briefly at the top with control, and repeat.
On rings, keep the rings close to the body so they do not drift wide. Difficulty increases if the feet are elevated or the straps are set lower so the body is more horizontal; a higher handle position makes it easier.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tuna2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2764/view/'),
  ('1463b53a-3901-414b-bbbf-2c3f86c8638c', 'TRX/Rings Y Raise', 'Shoulders', '- Set TRX straps or rings at about mid-chest to waist height. Stand facing the anchor and hold one handle or ring in each hand.
- Walk the feet toward the anchor and lean back until the arms are extended in front of you and the body forms a straight line from head to heels. Palms face each other or slightly down.
- Brace the core and glutes. Keep the shoulders down and the ribs stacked over the pelvis.
- With a soft bend in the elbows, raise the arms up and out into a Y shape overhead, so the hands finish roughly in line with the ears and wider than the shoulders.
- Reach long through the arms without shrugging. Pause briefly at the top with control.
- Lower the arms under control back to the start position in front of the body, then repeat.
Keep tension in the straps throughout. A more upright stance makes the movement easier; a deeper lean makes it harder.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tuna2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2765/view/'),
  ('9396a728-3da4-4390-ae4d-882395805008', 'Tuck L-sit', 'Abs', 'The tuck L-sit is a bodyweight hold where you support yourself on parallel bars, parallettes, or the floor with straight arms while pulling your knees toward your chest. Your hips stay lifted, your spine stays tall, and your feet hover off the ground. The goal is to keep your core tight, shoulders depressed, and arms locked out while maintaining the tucked position. It’s a core-intensive, shoulder-stabilizing static hold often used as a progression toward the full L-sit.', 'Bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1704/view/'),
  ('3e9973f6-c218-4dd4-850b-da6f4f1622bb', 'Tuck planche', 'Shoulders, Abs, Serratus anterior', 'Basic calisthenics progression for the full planche', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ISSACS', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1916/view/'),
  ('309bfd2b-1af4-49db-b64b-d7d7c7dd39bb', 'Turkish Get-Up', 'Shoulders, Glutes, Obliquus externus abdominis, Serratus anterior', 'Starting on back, move to the standing position with dumbbell in one hand.  Switch hands between reps.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'dookie1481', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/675/view/'),
  ('075bff87-f676-4d9d-92b4-39d557453fc7', 'Typewriter Pull-ups', 'Biceps, Lats', 'Hang from a pull-up bar with an overhand grip, slightly wider than shoulder-width. Pull yourself up until your chin is above the bar. At the top position, instead of going straight down, shift your body to one side by extending one arm while keeping the other arm bent. Move smoothly from one side to the other, like a typewriter motion, before lowering yourself back down. This exercise increases time under tension and strengthens the lats, biceps, and shoulders while building unilateral pulling strength.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tenebrizz', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1607/view/'),
  ('bc4bd5a0-46b5-4934-9a0a-dba2a9e5b2fa', 'Tyson push-ups', 'Chest, Triceps', 'Starting Position
• Feet: Place the soles of your feet flat against a vertical wall, with your toes or balls of your feet on the floor.
• Hands: Position your hands on the floor shoulder-width apart, directly under your lower chest or solar plexus.
• Body: Keep your core tight, glutes squeezed, and head in a neutral position. Your knees should hover a few inches above the floor without touching it.
Movement Pattern
- Lower Down: Flex your elbows (tucked around 45 degrees) to lower your chest toward the floor, stopping just a few centimeters short of touching the ground.
- Rock Back: As you press upward through your arms, push your hips backward and bend your knees, rocking your weight toward your heels while keeping your shins nearly parallel to the floor.
- Drive Forward: Explode forward from your lower-body/crouched position, shifting your weight back into the starting push-up plank stance to complete the repetition.
Key Tips for Proper Form
• Keep Knees Low: Do not let your knees or hips pike high up in the air; keep the motion low and horizontal.
• Maintain Wall Contact: Keep the soles of your feet pressed against the wall so you don''t lose your leverage.
• Engage the Core: Avoid arching your lower back by tilting your pelvis backward and bracing your abs.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'yahoo.ivory', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2679/view/'),
  ('76965848-9776-4be5-b879-e3c97033b80f', 'Underhand Lat Pull Down', 'Lats', 'Grip the pull-down bar with your palms facing you and your hands closer than shoulder-width apart. Lean back slightly and keep your back straight. Pull the bar down towards your chest, pulling your shoulders back slightly at the end of the motion.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/684/view/'),
  ('c1a9297d-6786-45dc-9047-5539fb61db9c', 'Unilateral Cable row', 'Lats, Serratus anterior', 'Put a vertical seated bench in front of the cable row machine.position it so that you can focus on one side, and then do slow pulls focusing on the lateral muscles and ensuring tention in those muscles when in a stretched position.', 'Bench, Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Rottekongen', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1621/view/'),
  ('d788811b-be46-485a-b8a0-0d9ba7f4cf85', 'unilateral cross body cable pull down', 'Lats', 'on a cable rack place cable high, and with a straight arm pull that cable across you from high to low. focus on tension in back shoulder.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1795/view/'),
  ('139e3d1a-ccd5-46cd-b953-8db6cea7b005', 'Unilateral Hip Thrust', 'Glutes', 'Extend one leg while laying down and start raising your body with one leg touching the ground through the heel.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cybro', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1913/view/'),
  ('2dd1cf54-b8e3-4769-9455-48120d7cc427', 'Unilateral Lunges', 'Glutes, Quads', 'No stops during movement, hands on the hips.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'cybro', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1907/view/'),
  ('df18b577-0610-4804-a993-161024da67db', 'Upper Back', 'Shoulders, Trapezius', 'Upper Back is suitable for building up the core muscles with a special focus on the deltoid and rhomboid muscles and the upper back muscles', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'phpi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1381/view/'),
  ('6fc4815a-8852-4e89-a10b-bd36a9029dbb', 'Upper External Oblique', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Exercise for upper external oblique muscles', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'http://www.carinatum.com/', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/688/view/'),
  ('01976085-6701-45ea-b152-5c46ba60550d', 'Upright Row w/ Dumbbells', 'Shoulders, Trapezius', 'Hold a dumbbell in each hand in front of your body. Keep your palms towards your body. Lift you hands straight up until your hands are under your chin, then lower them. Repeat the exercise.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'koreyhinton', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/694/view/'),
  ('738137c0-5387-4215-b457-ea7af113b3ba', 'Upright Row, on Multi Press', 'Shoulders, Trapezius', 'The movements are basically the same as with an SZ-bar, but you use the bar on the multi press:
Stand straight, your feet are shoulder-width apart. Hold the bar with an overhand grip on your thighs, the arms are stretched. Lift the bar close to the body till your chin. The elbows point out so that at the highest point they form a V. Make here a short pause before going slowly down and repeating the movement.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/691/view/'),
  ('5d40c67d-be59-4092-9c9c-301ca5310e2b', 'Upright Row, SZ-bar', 'Shoulders, Trapezius', 'Stand straight, your feet are shoulder-width apart. Hold the SZ-bar with an overhand grip on your thighs, the arms are stretched. Lift the bar close to the body till your chin. The elbows point out so that at the highest point they form a V. Make here a short pause before going slowly down and repeating the movement.', 'SZ-Bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'wger.de', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/693/view/'),
  ('d847301e-1613-4987-85cd-61d3ff9d7ef8', 'V-Bar Pulldown', 'Lats', 'Pulldowns using close grip v-bar.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'apeschel', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/695/view/'),
  ('e38c3858-5525-4ef5-9258-e1a3ab1ef034', 'V-Up', 'Abs', 'One of the best exercises for the rectus abdominis muscles. Lie on your back with your legs straight and your arms close to your sides. Lift your head off the floor and raise your legs and arms about 15 centimeters off the floor. Your palms should face down, and look up at the ceiling.
Now bend your legs and at the same time sit up so that your knees and chest almost touch. Only your buttocks are still on the floor. Your arms are now extended at shoulder height. Then slowly return to the starting position with your arms and legs extended and your head raised.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tobias08', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2689/view/'),
  ('36b6919a-0e9d-49c6-891b-28fb55ae88ad', 'Vpushup', 'Chest, Quads, Abs, Triceps', 'Lift your body off the ground by pushing your arms upwards', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1090/view/'),
  ('8b494562-012e-4944-be65-9b168f0c8c41', 'W-Raise', 'Obliquus externus abdominis, Abs', 'This challenging exercise is a variation of reverse crunches that is made up of three parts in which we’re basically drawing an upside down ‘W’ with our legs.
Start position is lying face up flat on the floor with legs extended at the low point of the outer leg of the ‘W’. Keeping a strong core and legs straight, go up, rise your hips and then slowly lower your legs down halfway. Then, lifting your legs back up to the top, rise your hips again, maintaining that straight line, use your core strength to finally come back all the way down to the other outer leg of the W. Then you reverse the ‘W’ to return to the start.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Moffi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1474/view/'),
  ('2e00d8e5-19a2-42ad-a954-f06a56f56561', 'Walking', 'Hamstrings, Calves, Glutes, Lats, Obliquus externus abdominis, Quads, Abs', 'Walking outdoor or indoor, try keeping a pace of at list 100 steps per minute.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', null, 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1104/view/'),
  ('f636ae1c-f678-48fe-96b3-1a9ae81f43ce', 'walking bridge', 'Abs', 'from a standing position with knees slightly bent and hands resting on the floor. From here, proceed forward with your hands keeping your buttocks contracted and without losing control of your lower back.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1103/view/'),
  ('2eb61587-85d1-494f-b2fb-a92dd108b3d3', 'Wall Angels', 'Trapezius', 'The Wall Angels exercise primarily targets the upper back and shoulder muscles, helping improve posture, shoulder mobility, and scapular control.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'bbayuwega', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1679/view/'),
  ('f20ab712-f953-4705-8710-9681392ddd07', 'Wall balls', 'Shoulders, Glutes, Quads', 'Get a medicine ball, shoulder width stance, squat, thrust the ball as high as possible against the wall and catch', 'Swiss Ball', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'philip', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1100/view/'),
  ('36a309ba-7dd1-43c9-9efc-788c7cd9fbd6', 'Wall Drills', 'Quads', 'Exercises for strengthening knee and leg musculature.
- Lateral Wall Drills March - https://youtu.be/9RiTlJ6Mmek
- Lateral Wall Drills OPEN - https://youtu.be/ADRlN8-Wfdg
- Lateral Wall Drills CROSS - https://youtu.be/hGH2sj0Tzu4', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Insight', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1196/view/'),
  ('0d68029a-f025-41fe-9c56-6ccbc32bc43d', 'Wall Handstand', null, 'Handstand against a wall for support (chest facing wall).', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Nash', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/711/view/'),
  ('51a80676-92d8-4f91-b51a-4666888e40db', 'Wall Pushup', 'Shoulders, Chest, Triceps', 'Pushup against a wall', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'Dexter', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/713/view/'),
  ('f1946fd4-793d-47a5-b66f-599b7f53695d', 'Wall Slides', 'Biceps, Hamstrings, Chest, Trapezius, Triceps', 'Stand with heels, shoulders, back of head, and hips touching the wall. Start with biceps straight out and elbows at a 90 degree angle. Straighten the arms while remaining againstthe wall without arching the back off of the wall, mimicking a shoulder press movement.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Whythebigpaws', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/716/view/'),
  ('46ee5805-512a-43a2-944c-97f7744b0078', 'Wall Squat', 'Hamstrings, Quads', 'Find a nice flat piece of wall and stand with your back leaning against the wall. Slowly slide down the wall while moving your feet away from it, until your thighs are parallel to the ground and both your knees and your hips are bent at a 90° angle. Cross your arms in front of your chest and hold this position for 30 seconds.
Variant: put a big inflated rubber ball (like a small basketball) between your knees and squeeze the ball while holding the squat position', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC0', 'Blablabla', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/718/view/'),
  ('9a5d36b8-7fa5-4705-8e9b-fafa211a13d6', 'Wall-Hold Rotation', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'Hold the pool wall or edge with one hand, body horizontal at the surface, legs kicking gently behind you. Keep your face in the water, exhaling continuously, then rotate your head to the side — not lifting it up — so one goggle stays in the water while the other clears the surface. Take a quick breath, then rotate your face back down to neutral. This isolates the head-rotation motion without any arm strokes to think about.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Imported from swim plan', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2483/view/'),
  ('3f8ad988-1aee-48ca-ad37-44e39ae1715d', 'Wall-sit', 'Glutes, Quads', 'The Wall Sit (or wall chair) is an isometric exercise that mainly involves the leg muscles, improving muscular endurance and stability. Here are the main and secondary muscles activated during the exercise.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'clafal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1408/view/'),
  ('8d333b94-148c-48f8-9154-8b13279500d1', 'Weighted Crunch', 'Obliquus externus abdominis', 'The Weighted Crunch is a variation of the classic crunch exercise that involves adding resistance (a weight) to increase the intensity of the abdominal work.
- Lay down
- (Optional) Bend your knees to add difficulty
- (Optional) Bend your knees and let your feet rest on a bench or a box or something (90 degree angle on knees)
- Lift your head and torso while bending your back forward (if you don''t it''s a sit-up and also involves some back muscles). The higher you go, the more you should feel your abs contracting.
- Go back to starting position an repeat', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'AlucardEvil40', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1648/view/'),
  ('0d245efd-7d9a-4518-be17-cce14adae88a', 'Weighted neck flexion', 'Trapezius', 'Weighted neck flexion is an isolation exercise designed to strengthen the anterior (front) neck flexor muscles by bringing your chin toward your chest against external resistance (most commonly a light weight plate, resistance band, or neck harness).', 'Bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'qoxivi', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2676/view/'),
  ('37abbe32-2fab-4818-8355-fffcff2dc3e3', 'Weighted push-ups', 'Chest', '- Setup: Begin in a standard plank position with a weight plate balanced securely on your upper back.
- Alignment: Maintain a straight line from your head to your heels, engaging your core to prevent your back from sagging.
- Execution: Lower your body until your chest nearly touches the floor, keeping your elbows at a forty-five degrees.
- Ascent: Press through your palms to return to the starting position, fully extending your arms.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'nishant0712', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1902/view/'),
  ('591992cb-48ee-4b39-b790-e05b4a2c11e3', 'Weighted Step-ups', 'Glutes, Quads', 'box step ups w/ barbell and 45''s on each side', null, 'wger', 'CC0', 'jigglychipmunk', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/722/view/'),
  ('1842e1be-ac67-4f34-a527-4ca2b72d8e11', 'Wide Push-Up', 'Chest', '​A push-up variation with a wider hand placement to increase the emphasis on the outer chest muscles and shoulders.
- ​Setup: Place hands wider than shoulder-width apart on the floor.
- Lower: Descend until your chest nearly touches the floor, keeping your core tight.
- ​Push: Drive through your palms to return to the start position.
- ​Form: Don''t let your lower back sag; keep a straight line from head to heels.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Anon#2', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1964/view/'),
  ('55b9d286-c4cc-4a29-97ad-58cb13c2bb7e', 'Wide-grip Pulldown', 'Lats', '-
Starting position:
- Arms extended overhead, feeling a stretch in your lats.
- Keep your shoulders depressed (don’t shrug upward).
-
Pulling phase (concentric):
- Pull the bar down to your upper chest or collarbone area by driving your elbows down and back.
- Keep your chest lifted and squeeze your shoulder blades together at the bottom.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 3', 'tuckerm', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/723/view/'),
  ('cc9417c7-5004-42d1-9432-df5dda2fe1da', 'Wide-grip Pulldown (Custom)', 'Lats', 'Perform Wide-grip Pulldown with controlled form, keeping tension on the target muscles, pausing briefly at the peak contraction, then returning slowly under control without using momentum.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'ruochenma', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2713/view/'),
  ('d33f3b19-436f-48a2-aff6-946582c94bdb', 'Wide-grip supinated lat pulldown', 'Lats', 'Downward movement:
- Pull the bar down so that it passes close to your chin and touches the upper part of your chest.
- Keep the rest of your body still.', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Franpol', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1125/view/'),
  ('026d2939-9156-408b-bf11-21cfa8278851', 'Windshield Wipers', 'Obliquus externus abdominis, Abs', 'Hang from a pull-up bar, raise your legs to the ceiling. Move lower body from left to right with straight legs like a windshield wiper', 'Pull-up bar', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Happy', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1743/view/'),
  ('a5828b61-2f5c-42a0-a085-71b91d18ecc3', 'Wrist circles', null, 'Wrist circles is warm-up exercise, where you rotate your wrists in a circular motion. They improve mobility and are recommended before exercises such as pull ups, hand stands and other exercises that put pressure on your wrists.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'Awlex', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2543/view/'),
  ('f6471f4a-0290-4a71-9580-bf3d046fdb9d', 'Wrist curl, cable', null, '- Extend your arm down, then slightly lift your forearm
- Extend your wrist in a supinated form (palm up)
- Adjust cable height so that the cable handle is in your supinated hand
- Perform the movement by
curling your palms and wrists upwards
- (optional/variable) Pause at the top
- Slowly return to the starting position', 'Cable machine', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'eufvksruh', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1771/view/'),
  ('2cc92db8-b245-44b8-b1d6-bf91c78c2ab9', 'Wrist curl, dumbbells', null, '- Sitting on a bench, grab the dumbbell with your palms facing up
and your hands shoulder-width apart. Rest your forearms on your thighs
and allow your wrists to hang over your knees.
- Perform the movement by
curling your palms and wrists towards your face.
- (optional/variable) Pause at the top.
- Slowly return to the starting position.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'wakanda90', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1205/view/'),
  ('17eb3e8d-26b7-4bb8-8080-cb6550867ce4', 'Wrist rocks', null, 'Start on all fours with your palms flat on the floor. Slowly rock forward and backward, keeping your hands planted. Move only within a comfortable, pain-free wrist range to gently improve mobility and flexibility.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'nalhilal', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2680/view/'),
  ('4229860d-b84d-4bd9-ac92-3e06012e0d07', 'Wrist Roller', null, 'Hold a wrist roller, a short bar with a rope and a small weight attached, with both hands at shoulder height and arms straight out in front. Roll the bar with your wrists to wind the rope up until the weight reaches the bar, then unwind it slowly. Roll forward and backward on different sets to train both sides of the forearm.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'lightking', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2740/view/'),
  ('20152760-c624-4775-a3ff-8ef32d2cd545', 'Wrist Stretch', null, 'Extend your right arm straight out in front of you, with your palm facing up (fingers pointing toward the floor). Using your left hand, gently pull the fingers of your right hand toward you until you feel a stretch in your forearm and biceps.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tobias08', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2686/view/'),
  ('8ca01d32-2e86-49a1-a130-b99219432e42', 'Yoga exercise: Cow-cat', 'Shoulders, Lats, Trapezius', 'First get into the four-footed stance. The hands are underneath the shoulders. The fingers are fanned out wide and ensure a stable stance. It is best to rest your front body weight on your thumbs and index fingers. Now place your legs hip-width apart on the yoga mat. Your thighs should be vertically below your hips. Make sure that your weight is evenly distributed between your hands and knees. Your head is an extension of your spine and you are looking down at your mat. Your back is in a neutral position. Breathe in deeply and start with the cat. With the next exhalation, round your back vertebra by vertebra. Try to pull yourself as far as possible towards the ceiling. Pull your head towards your chest and tilt your pelvis. Now inhale deeply and move into the opposite position, cow pose. Bend your back down, pull your shoulders back slightly and lift your head as far as is comfortable for you. Your gaze is directed upwards. Breathe out consciously and switch back to the cat. With the next exhalation, switch back to cow. Repeat the exercise a few times and make sure that you are in the flow.', 'Gym mat', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'damnlost', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1244/view/'),
  ('142fcef5-63a7-4d30-81e4-bc9d7073fe99', 'YTW Raises', 'Shoulders', 'The YTW exercise for scapular retraction is designed to strengthen the inter-scapular muscles between your shoulder blades, to help retract or pull your shoulders back, helping to maintain proper upright posture.', 'none (bodyweight exercise)', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'probablyforging', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/2454/view/'),
  ('5986cd29-11ff-43f4-af65-fc48dec62156', 'YTWL Exercise', 'Shoulders, Trapezius', '- Purpose: Improves shoulder stability, posture, and scapular (shoulder blade) control. Often used in physical therapy and warm-ups.
- Starting Position:
Stand with feet shoulder-width apart. Hinge forward at the hips (like a
deadlift) keeping your back flat, or lie face down on an incline bench.
Keep your thumbs pointing up throughout.
- Y (Arms up):
Extend both arms straight forward and upward at a 135-degree angle from
your body, forming a "Y". Squeeze your shoulder blades together at the
top.
- T (Arms out): Open your arms straight out to the sides, perpendicular to your body, forming a "T". Focus on pinching your shoulder blades.
- W (Arms bent): Pull your elbows back, bending them to 90 degrees, and squeeze your shoulder blades to form a "W". Keep your wrists firm.
- L (Arms rotated):
Start with elbows bent at 90 degrees and close to your body. Rotate
your forearms outward (external rotation) like a goalpost, forming an
"L" or a gate.
- Key Focus:
Movements should be slow and controlled. Always lead with the thumbs
and think about moving the shoulder blades, not just the arms.', 'Dumbbell, Incline bench', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'teus_ergaster', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1885/view/'),
  ('981b07b6-e76e-438c-bad4-388441b7ff33', 'YWTs', null, 'Starting position:
Lie down, preferably on a mat. Stretch your arms above your head in a "Y" position (arms pointing up diagonally at around 45 degrees). Your thumbs should be pointing up.
Steps:
- With arms in the Y position, lift your chest slightly from the ground, creating a pull tension in your arms upward.
- Hold this position for several seconds (15–30 is ideal).
- Keeping your chest up, bend at your elbows to move from a Y position to a "W" position, with arms bent at the elbows and maintaining the upward tension.
- Hold for around as long as with the Y position.
- Still with your chest up, move to a "T" position, straightening your arms out to the side to be perpendicular to your body.
- Hold for around as long as you held the W position.
- Move from the T position to the W position, then from the W position to the Y position.
- Repeat.', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'tinman', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1083/view/'),
  ('89c3d614-e841-4c04-bee8-6761567e4b7c', 'Zone 2 Running', null, 'Zone two Cardio for endurance, you should be able to speak while running', null, 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'karly', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/908/view/'),
  ('9564ab2d-2534-4b47-bdf7-f2f653aae3de', 'Zottman curl', 'Biceps, Brachialis', 'With your palms facing forward, curl the weights up to your shoulders. Turn your hands so that that your palms downwards and slowly return the weights back down. Finally face your palms back forward.', 'Dumbbell', 'wger', 'CC-BY-SA 4', 'meldun', 'https://wger.de/en/exercise/1683/view/')
on conflict do nothing;

